### Single Evaluation of the Left-Hand Side

In compound assignments such as `E1 -= E2`, Java guarantees that the left-hand operand expression `E1` is **evaluated exactly once**:

```java
int[] arr = { 10, 20, 30 };
int i = 1;
arr[i++] -= 5; 
// i++ is evaluated ONCE to determine the index (index 1)
// arr[1] becomes 15, and i becomes 2.
```

If this were written in naive expanded form `arr[i++] = arr[i++] - 5`, `i++` would be evaluated twice, which would corrupt array indices and produce unintended side effects. Java's compound assignment protects against this.

