### Why You Should Never Use `==` on Floating-Point Values

Because floating-point numbers in Java use binary fractions (IEEE 754), many decimal numbers (like 0.1 and 0.2) cannot be represented with exact precision:

```java
double sum = 0.1 + 0.2;
System.out.println(sum == 0.3); // Prints: false!
System.out.println(sum);        // Prints: 0.30000000000000004
```

#### Best Practice for Float Comparison:
Compare the absolute difference against a small threshold (epsilon `ε`):
```java
final double EPSILON = 1e-9;
if (Math.abs(a - b) < EPSILON) {
    // Numbers are considered equal
}
```

